(() => {
'use strict'; const E=window.CANON_EXPAND;if(!E)throw Error('Expansion core missing'); const add=E.add;

add({unit:9,id:'atonement-images',title:'Atonement: several images of one saving work',reading:'2 Corinthians 5:18–21',ref:[47,5,18,21],objective:'Distinguish reconciliation, substitution, and victory without reducing the cross to a moral example.',body:[
'This passage presents reconciliation as God’s initiative through Christ. Reconciliation concerns a relationship restored across estrangement. Its direction matters: the saving work is not described as something humanity first achieves by making itself worthy.',
'Paul also speaks of sin and righteousness in relation to Christ and those reconciled through him. Christians draw on this and other passages when explaining representation, substitution, sacrifice, and exchange. A theological model gathers evidence; it is not identical to a single metaphor.',
'Penal substitution describes Christ as bearing the penalty due to sinners. Victory accounts emphasize the defeat of sin and death; reconciliation emphasizes restored relationship. These themes can overlap. The guide accommodates penal substitution while declining to present it as the only scriptural account of how salvation is accomplished.',
'Christ’s necessary sacrifice and resurrection remain central. The lesson concerns what Christ accomplishes; the next lesson considers receiving the gift. Keeping these questions distinct protects the saving work from being reduced either to a transaction diagram or to an inspiring example humanity could reproduce on its own.'
],simple:'Reconciliation names restored relationship; penal substitution names bearing a penalty; victory names defeating hostile powers. Each highlights a dimension of Christ’s saving work.',vocab:[['Atonement','Christ’s saving work addressing sin and reconciliation with God.'],['Penal substitution','An account in which Christ bears the penalty due to sinners.'],['Reconciliation','The restoration of relationship across estrangement.']],deeper:'Compare this passage with Colossians 2:13–15 in the overview. State what each image highlights and what requires a wider theological argument.',reflect:'Which question does reconciliation answer, and which question might another image illuminate?',model:'Reconciliation helps describe the relationship being restored. Victory helps describe sin and death as powers overcome; neither image needs to erase the other.',evidence:['The passage locates reconciliation in God’s work through Christ.','It connects Christ’s work with sin and righteousness.','The passage presents reconciliation as a human achievement completed before God acts.','One metaphor by itself explains every aspect of atonement.'],claim:'God’s saving initiative through Christ is described through relational and sin-bearing language.',application:'Explain which question an atonement image answers and where a wider argument is needed.',decisions:[['A learner says different atonement images must be mutually exclusive. What helps?','Identify the different questions each image illuminates.','Choose one word and discard every other biblical image.','Images can overlap without being interchangeable or exhaustive.'],['Someone says the cross is only a lesson in being kind. What is missing?','Its saving significance in addressing sin and estrangement.','A requirement that humans first achieve reconciliation unaided.','The passage attributes reconciliation to God’s work through Christ.']]});

add({unit:9,id:'receiving-grace',title:'Receiving grace: repentance and trust',reading:'Ephesians 2:1–10',ref:[49,2,1,10],objective:'Distinguish receiving salvation from earning it and human dependence from lack of human value.',body:[
'Ephesians moves from a description of bondage and death toward God’s mercy and life with Christ. The movement gives divine initiative priority. It does not portray salvation as the final prize awarded to people who have already repaired themselves.',
'Grace names the gift-character of salvation. Faith names trust and reception rather than a competing currency with which to purchase it. In this guide, acknowledging sin, repentance, and acceptance of Christ’s saving gift belong to receiving grace, not to establishing a claim that God owes salvation.',
'The denial of boasting addresses the attempt to make salvation a measure of superiority. Inability to merit salvation must not be turned into the claim that people have no value. The text’s appeal to love and mercy belongs within the explanation, not outside it.',
'The movement toward good works remains part of the passage. The order matters: created for a changed life is not the same as saved by accumulating achievements. When explaining the gospel, separate the gift, its reception, and its ethical result while keeping their relationship visible.'
],simple:'Grace is a gift, faith receives it, and changed conduct follows. Receiving help does not mean a person has no value.',vocab:[['Grace','God’s freely given favor and saving gift.'],['Faith','Trust in and reception of God’s saving work through Christ.'],['Merit','A claim to deserve a reward on the basis of achievement.']],deeper:'Ask what boasting would imply about the source of salvation. Then ask why the passage still speaks of good works after denying that salvation is earned by works.',reflect:'How would you explain dependence on grace without humiliating someone?',model:'I would say that none of us can accomplish salvation for ourselves, while emphasizing that God’s love gives people value rather than erasing it.',evidence:['The passage describes salvation as God’s gift.','Good works appear as a purpose of the changed life.','A sufficiently large record of achievements makes salvation owed.','Depending on grace means a person lacks all value.'],claim:'Salvation is received as grace, with good works as a resulting vocation rather than its purchase price.',application:'Explain repentance and trust as receiving a gift, without turning them into grounds for boasting.',decisions:[['A learner asks how many good deeds purchase salvation. What needs correction?','The assumption that grace is purchased by a tally.','Only the number of deeds on the tally.','The passage denies that works establish a basis for boasting in salvation.'],['Someone concludes that a gift makes conduct irrelevant. What follows in the passage?','A life directed toward good works.','A statement that no action can matter after receiving grace.','The text rejects earning salvation while still giving changed conduct a purpose.']]});

add({unit:9,id:'grace-repair',title:'After forgiveness: love takes practical form',reading:'Luke 19:1–10',ref:[42,19,1,10],objective:'Distinguish a response to grace from payment for welcome and salvation.',body:[
'The Zacchaeus narrative joins seeking, encounter, welcome, complaint, and concrete concern about possessions and wrongdoing. Read the sequence before turning it into an abstract formula. Jesus’ approach and the crowd’s response are part of the meaning.',
'Zacchaeus speaks about giving and repayment. Readers debate aspects of the statement’s tense and implications, but its ethical content is clear: money and harm are not irrelevant to the encounter. The story concerns more than an inward feeling detached from neighbors.',
'This guide treats repair as a response to grace, not a payment that forces God to save. Repentance is more credible when it acknowledges those harmed. Forgiveness also does not give an offender the right to demand instant trust or unrestricted access to someone they have hurt.',
'A practical response can involve admission, restitution where possible, changed habits, and respect for boundaries. Different harms require different forms of repair. The lesson supplies a theological connection between grace and conduct, not a promise that every damaged relationship can be restored on demand.'
],simple:'Grace and practical repair belong together. Making amends is a response to the gift, not a way to buy it.',vocab:[['Restitution','Returning what was taken or compensating for a concrete loss where possible.'],['Repentance','Turning from wrongdoing in attitude and conduct.'],['Boundary','A limit that can protect people even when forgiveness is being considered.']],deeper:'Read the full encounter before treating one statement as a universal formula. Distinguish forgiveness, repaired material loss, and rebuilt trust; these are related but not identical.',reflect:'What makes an apology more than a request for relief from guilt?',model:'It should acknowledge the harm and consider what repair the affected person needs. I cannot make their immediate trust a condition of my willingness to change.',evidence:['The encounter includes concern about giving and repayment.','Jesus’ welcome provokes a complaint from others.','Forgiveness automatically requires an injured person to restore trust immediately.','Material harm is irrelevant when a person uses religious words.'],claim:'An encounter with grace has consequences for relationships and the handling of harm.',application:'Pair an acknowledgment of wrongdoing with a concrete form of repair that respects the affected person.',decisions:[['Someone apologizes but demands immediate trust. What distinction helps?','Forgiveness and rebuilt trust are not identical.','A religious apology cancels every protective boundary.','Repair includes changed conduct and respect for the other person’s needs.'],['A learner calls restitution the fee for salvation. What needs clarifying?','Repair is a response to grace rather than its purchase price.','The story should be read without any concern for conduct.','The guide keeps the saving gift distinct from the ethical response it invites.']]});

add({unit:10,id:'acts-discernment',title:'Acts 15: a community works through disagreement',reading:'Acts 15:1–21',ref:[44,15,1,21],objective:'Trace testimony, Scripture, and communal reasoning in a dispute about Gentile belonging.',body:[
'The dispute concerns what is required of Gentile believers. Acts does not describe a community free from conflict or difficult questions about inherited practice. The disagreement is serious enough to require a shared process of discernment.',
'The account includes debate, testimony about God’s work, and an appeal to Scripture. These elements should be identified separately before describing how they work together. A conclusion is not merely announced without the narrative giving reasons for it.',
'The resolution addresses a specific community and question. It should not be treated as a blank authorization for any later proposal, nor as proof that every modern disagreement is identical to this one. An analogy requires both similarities and differences.',
'For contemporary discussion, practice stating the actual question, hearing evidence, representing opposing concerns fairly, and explaining the decision’s scope. Disagreement need not be hidden for a community to learn, but a process should remain attentive to the people whose belonging is being discussed.'
],simple:'The community reasons through a real dispute. Testimony, Scripture, and the question being asked all matter.',vocab:[['Gentile','A person who is not Jewish.'],['Discernment','Shared or individual judgment exercised with evidence and responsibility.'],['Analogy','A comparison that must account for differences as well as similarities.']],deeper:'List the types of reasons used before drawing a modern parallel. Which elements belong specifically to the question of Gentile inclusion in this account?',reflect:'How could a disagreement be made more precise before people argue over its answer?',model:'I could state the actual proposed requirement and ask what reasons support it. That keeps a debate from shifting among several unrelated questions.',evidence:['The account presents a dispute concerning Gentile believers.','The discussion includes testimony and an appeal to Scripture.','A later proposal is correct simply because someone compares it with Acts 15.','The early community never experienced disagreement.'],claim:'Communal discernment brings evidence and Scripture to a clearly identified question.',application:'State a disputed question precisely and explain how the evidence bears on it.',decisions:[['A group argues without agreeing what requirement is disputed. What comes first?','State the concrete question and who is affected.','Vote on a slogan before clarifying its meaning.','A reasoned decision needs an identifiable question.'],['Someone calls a present dispute exactly the same as Acts 15. What is needed?','Explain relevant similarities and differences.','Treat the comparison itself as sufficient proof.','An analogy cannot replace examination of a new question’s actual features.']],sequence:['Reconstruct the broad argument movement in Acts 15:1–21.',['A requirement for Gentile believers is disputed.','The community hears discussion and testimony.','Scripture is brought into the reasoning.','A proposal addresses the disputed requirement.'],'Follow the problem, the reasons, and the proposed response.']});

add({unit:10,id:'body-belonging',title:'One body: difference without disposable people',reading:'1 Corinthians 12:12–27',ref:[46,12,12,27],objective:'Explain Paul’s body image as an argument for interdependence and care.',body:[
'The body image holds unity and difference together. Paul does not solve difference by requiring every member to perform the same function. Neither does he treat distinct functions as a reason for some people to be excluded from belonging.',
'The argument challenges two directions of exclusion: a member declaring itself outside because it differs, and another declaring that a member is unnecessary. Both fail to understand the body’s interdependence. The image concerns mutual need as well as identity.',
'Paul gives particular attention to members treated as weaker or less honorable. The argument therefore does more than celebrate impressive talents. It asks how the community regards those who may be easily overlooked, and how suffering and honor are shared.',
'A modern application might examine whose participation is constrained by inaccessible arrangements or whose work goes unnoticed. The passage is not a complete organizational chart. Its argument supplies a reason to question arrangements that treat people as disposable.'
],simple:'Belonging does not require sameness. The body needs its different members and must attend to those it tends to overlook.',vocab:[['Interdependence','Mutual need and connection among people with different roles.'],['Metaphor','An image that illuminates a relationship without making every detail identical.'],['Belonging','Being recognized as part of the community rather than as disposable.']],deeper:'Identify the problem each imagined body part voices. Do not convert the metaphor into a hierarchy that contradicts its attention to overlooked members.',reflect:'Whose contribution might a group fail to notice, and how could that change?',model:'A quiet member may offer care that receives little public recognition. I can ask how the group’s habits acknowledge that contribution and support participation.',evidence:['The body contains members with different functions.','Paul challenges the claim that another member is unnecessary.','Unity requires everyone to perform an identical role.','Only publicly impressive members deserve care.'],claim:'Unity involves mutual dependence and particular care for people a community might overlook.',application:'Identify and address an arrangement that treats a participant as unnecessary or invisible.',decisions:[['A member thinks a less visible role means they do not belong. What does the image challenge?','The equation of difference with exclusion.','The existence of any different functions.','Paul’s body includes different functions without making difference a reason for non-belonging.'],['A leader calls some people expendable because they are less productive. What should be examined?','How the community understands care and mutual need.','Only whether replacing those people is efficient.','The argument attends to members who might otherwise be treated as less honorable.']]});

add({unit:10,id:'letters-favoritism',title:'Letters: reading a correction as a correction',reading:'James 2:1–9',ref:[59,2,1,9],objective:'Distinguish the behavior a letter reports from the behavior it endorses.',body:[
'James imagines or describes an assembly giving different treatment to people on the basis of visible wealth. The contrast is vivid enough to reveal how ordinary gestures can establish who is valued. A seating decision can express a social judgment.',
'The letter criticizes favoritism rather than recommending the scene. This is a basic but important reading distinction: Scripture may describe a harmful practice in order to confront it. Isolating the scene from the author’s evaluation reverses the argument.',
'The appeal to loving one’s neighbor gives the criticism its ethical direction. The issue is not solved by a declaration that everyone is welcome if actual conduct still honors wealth and humiliates poverty. The stated principle must be examined against observable practice.',
'When reading other letters, identify the addressee, the issue, the reasons, and the requested response. A correction tells us something about a problem, but not necessarily its prevalence in every early Christian community. Stay within the evidence before generalizing.'
],simple:'A letter can show a bad practice to challenge it. Look for the author’s judgment, not just the scene being described.',vocab:[['Epistle','A letter, often addressing a community and particular concerns.'],['Favoritism','Unequal treatment based on status rather than fair regard.'],['Authorial evaluation','The judgment the writer makes about a reported action.']],deeper:'Ask how much historical information a correction supplies. It can identify a concern without proving that every congregation behaved in precisely the same way.',reflect:'What observable practice would make a claim of equal welcome credible?',model:'I would examine how newcomers are greeted and whether money affects whose needs receive attention. The principle needs an observable expression.',evidence:['James describes different treatment based on visible wealth.','The letter criticizes favoritism through its argument.','A practice becomes endorsed merely because a letter describes it.','A correction proves every early congregation had an identical problem.'],claim:'The reported scene is evidence for a critique of favoritism, not an instruction to reproduce it.',application:'Compare a community’s stated welcome with how it actually treats people of different means.',decisions:[['A reader cites the seating scene as a recommended arrangement. What should they read next?','The author’s evaluation and argument.','Only the description of clothing.','The letter’s criticism determines how the scene functions.'],['A group advertises equal welcome but privileges donors. What should be evaluated?','Its observable treatment of people.','Only the wording of the advertisement.','James examines conduct that can contradict a stated commitment.']]});

add({unit:11,id:'trinity-language',title:'Trinity: one God, real distinction',reading:'Matthew 28:16–20',ref:[40,28,16,20],objective:'Distinguish the historic Trinity from three gods, three parts, and one person changing roles.',body:[
'The closing commission names Father, Son, and Holy Spirit together in baptism. Read what the passage says before moving to later terminology. Historic Trinitarian teaching draws this testimony together with other biblical passages rather than pretending a complete later definition appears in one sentence.',
'The historic confession holds that God is one and that Father, Son, and Spirit are distinct persons. Person here does not simply mean three separate human individuals enlarged to cosmic size. The language protects distinction without dividing God into three competing beings.',
'Calling each person one third of God fails to express the confession. Describing one person changing costumes also fails, because it removes real distinction. These are useful near-miss comparisons: identifying why an explanation fails can be clearer than adding an elaborate analogy.',
'A beginner can learn the boundaries of the teaching without claiming to make God fully comprehensible. Mystery names the limits of understanding; it should not be used to avoid distinguishing a coherent formulation from its alternatives. The mission checks that distinction, not personal assent.'
],simple:'Historic Christianity confesses one God and three distinct persons. It does not mean three gods, three pieces, or one person in three costumes.',vocab:[['Trinity','The historic confession of one God in three persons.'],['Distinction','A real difference that need not imply separation into three gods.'],['Doctrinal synthesis','A formulation drawing several biblical testimonies together.']],deeper:'An analogy can illustrate one feature while distorting another. State which feature it helps explain and exactly where it fails.',reflect:'How would you correct a misleading analogy without ridiculing the learner?',model:'I could say that the costume image conveys unity but loses the distinction of persons. I would return to the definition rather than keep adding costumes.',evidence:['The commission names Father, Son, and Holy Spirit together.','Baptism and teaching are placed within the commission.','This passage explicitly defines three separate gods.','A familiar analogy automatically supplies a complete definition of God.'],claim:'The passage contributes relational and baptismal testimony to a wider doctrinal synthesis.',application:'Explain the historic formulation and name one limit of an analogy used to illustrate it.',decisions:[['Someone describes the Trinity as three equal slices of God. What needs correction?','The suggestion that each person is only part of God.','Only whether the slices are precisely equal.','The historic confession does not divide God into components.'],['A learner says mystery means no distinction can be evaluated. What is a better response?','Acknowledge limits while comparing claims carefully.','Treat every contradictory explanation as equally accurate.','Limited comprehension does not erase the difference between a teaching and an alternative.']]});

add({unit:11,id:'incarnation-humanity',title:'Incarnation: fully divine and fully human',reading:'John 1:1–18',ref:[43,1,1,18],objective:'Explain incarnation without reducing Jesus to a divine disguise or a half-human mixture.',body:[
'John begins with the Word in relation to God and then speaks of the Word becoming flesh. The movement holds divine identity and embodied life together. Flesh should not be treated as an unimportant outer costume in this account.',
'Historic Christianity confesses Jesus as fully divine and fully human. The claim is not that two incomplete halves combine to form an unusual third kind of being. Nor does it mean the human life is only a visual performance without genuine human experience.',
'The passage links Jesus with grace, truth, and making God known. Incarnation concerns revelation as well as presence: Christians look to Jesus to understand God’s character. The course reads human life, suffering, and self-giving in light of this confession.',
'Compare John’s language with Philippians 2:5–11 in the unit overview. Ask what each presentation emphasizes before constructing a summary. A disciplined explanation preserves both humanity and divinity instead of using one to cancel the other whenever a question becomes difficult.'
],simple:'Incarnation means the Son truly becomes human. Historic teaching keeps both full humanity and full divinity in view.',vocab:[['Incarnation','The Son’s becoming human in Jesus Christ.'],['Revelation','Making known what would otherwise remain hidden.'],['Nature','In this doctrinal discussion, what is meant by being divine or human.']],deeper:'Distinguish what John explicitly narrates from later terminology used to synthesize it. An explanation should preserve the passage’s claims without pretending to settle every philosophical question.',reflect:'What is lost if Jesus’ humanity is treated as a disguise?',model:'His embodied life would cease to be genuinely human. I would keep that loss visible when explaining why the historic confession rejects the disguise model.',evidence:['John connects the Word with God at the opening.','The passage says the Word became flesh.','Human embodiment is presented merely as an irrelevant costume.','The historic formulation means Jesus is only half human.'],claim:'The passage holds divine identity and embodied human life together.',application:'Explain incarnation in a way that preserves both full humanity and full divinity.',decisions:[['A learner describes Jesus as God wearing a temporary human mask. What is obscured?','The reality of his human life.','Only the appearance of the mask.','The historic confession affirms genuine humanity, not merely its outward appearance.'],['Someone proposes a half-divine, half-human formula. What should replace it?','Fully divine and fully human, with the limits of our explanation acknowledged.','A more precise percentage for each half.','The traditional formulation does not treat either nature as an incomplete ingredient.']]});

add({unit:11,id:'spirit-discernment',title:'The Spirit: help, character, and discernment',reading:'Galatians 5:16–26',ref:[48,5,16,26],objective:'Connect teaching about the Spirit with observable character while avoiding certainty based only on intensity of feeling.',body:[
'Galatians describes conflict in human conduct and contrasts destructive works with the fruit of the Spirit. The metaphor of fruit directs attention to what a life produces. It is not merely an inventory of dramatic spiritual experiences.',
'The listed qualities include love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control. Different translations use different English terms, so compare wording without assuming the moral concern changes whenever a word changes.',
'The warning against conceit, provocation, and envy keeps the passage communal. Spiritual language does not excuse contempt for neighbors. A claim to guidance should be examined alongside conduct, responsibility, and the wider scriptural conversation.',
'This does not turn a list of virtues into a salvation score or make every difficult emotion a spiritual failure. Growth can include admitting harm, seeking help, and practicing a different response. The Spirit’s work is taught alongside human weakness and dependence, including Romans 8 in this unit.'
],simple:'Look at what a claimed spiritual influence produces in conduct. Intensity of feeling alone does not establish its source.',vocab:[['Fruit of the Spirit','The qualities of life associated with the Spirit in Galatians 5.'],['Self-control','Responsible regulation of one’s actions rather than domination of others.'],['Discernment','Testing a claim with attention to its content and effects.']],deeper:'Compare the fruit list with the final warning about conceit and envy. The list should not be used as a reason to boast about spiritual superiority.',reflect:'Which quality could become one observable practice in a difficult conversation?',model:'Patience could mean listening through a person’s explanation before responding. I can evaluate that practice without pretending never to feel frustration.',evidence:['The passage names qualities such as patience and gentleness.','It also warns against conceit, provocation, and envy.','A powerful feeling automatically proves divine guidance.','The virtues are a price list for earning salvation.'],claim:'Teaching about the Spirit is connected with character and communal conduct.',application:'Test a claimed spiritual insight against its content, consequences, and treatment of others.',decisions:[['Someone says a strong feeling authorizes humiliating a neighbor. What should be examined?','The claim’s consistency with the passage’s concern for conduct.','Only how intense the feeling was.','Intensity cannot cancel the ethical questions raised by the claimed guidance.'],['A learner struggles with patience and assumes the mission judges their salvation. What is being assessed?','Understanding the teaching and a possible practice.','Their ultimate spiritual worth.','The course’s task checks understanding; virtues are not a salvation score.']]});

add({unit:11,id:'providence-freedom',title:'Providence: purpose without erasing response',reading:'Romans 8:26–30',ref:[45,8,26,30],objective:'Distinguish providence, foreknowledge, and determinism within the guide’s account of meaningful response.',body:[
'Romans places God’s help within weakness and hope. The passage does not begin with humans understanding every event. The Spirit’s intercession is part of its account of divine involvement in lives that remain limited and vulnerable.',
'The claim about God working for good must not be turned into the claim that every harmful act is itself good. The following language relates God’s purpose to conformity to the Son. A promise about divine purpose is not permission to call cruelty necessary or desirable.',
'Foreknowledge concerns knowing beforehand; predestination concerns an appointed end; determinism concerns what determines events. Christians relate these differently. This guide teaches meaningful acceptance or resistance to grace while affirming that human choices cannot ultimately thwart God’s purpose.',
'Knowing an outcome and causing every choice are different claims. Likewise, God bringing good from an event does not remove an actor’s responsibility for harm. Explain these distinctions without claiming the passage gives a complete philosophical model or a specific diagnosis of every tragedy.'
],simple:'God’s purpose and human responsibility are both taken seriously. Knowing, causing, and bringing good from an event are different claims.',vocab:[['Providence','God’s care and purposes within the world and history.'],['Foreknowledge','Knowing beforehand, a concept distinct from causing.'],['Determinism','An account in which events are determined by prior causes or conditions.']],deeper:'Compare the passage’s stated purpose with the outcomes readers sometimes promise on its behalf. It does not guarantee that every personal preference will be fulfilled.',reflect:'How would you preserve hope without calling someone’s harm good?',model:'I could affirm the possibility of healing and new good while still naming the original action as wrong and supporting accountability.',evidence:['The passage speaks of help within weakness.','It relates God’s purpose to conformity to the Son.','Every harmful act becomes morally good if good later emerges.','Foreknowledge and causing a choice are identical definitions.'],claim:'Divine purpose offers hope without turning harm into goodness or erasing responsibility.',application:'Hold hope for healing together with accountability for actual harm.',decisions:[['Someone excuses cruelty because good later followed. What distinction matters?','Good brought from harm does not make the harm good.','Any later benefit retroactively justifies the action.','Hope and moral evaluation address related but different questions.'],['A reader equates foreknowledge with forcing every choice. What should be clarified?','Knowing and causing are distinct claims.','The two words are simply interchangeable.','Theological systems relate them differently; the definitions should not be collapsed.']]});

add({unit:12,id:'baptism-belonging',title:'Baptism: sign, participation, and community',reading:'Romans 6:1–4',ref:[45,6,1,4],objective:'Explain baptism’s connection with Christ and fairly distinguish infant and believer practices.',body:[
'Paul connects baptism with Christ’s death and resurrection in an argument about a changed life. The passage’s imagery is stronger than describing an arbitrary membership badge. It locates the baptized person’s identity in relation to Christ.',
'Christians differ over how baptism relates to grace, faith, and salvation. Some emphasize sacramental participation; others emphasize a confession of faith and symbolic testimony. The guide teaches its spiritual and communal significance without declaring baptism a necessary condition for salvation.',
'Infant-baptizing communities commonly emphasize divine initiative and nurture within the community. Believer-baptizing communities emphasize a person’s own profession of faith. These are different reasons and practices, not evidence that one side cares about faith while the other does not.',
'A beginner can ask what promises are spoken, what the community undertakes, and how the practice is understood locally. Learning those details should make an actual conversation with a church more informed. It should not become a test that excludes someone from this course or its welcome.'
],simple:'Baptism connects Christian belonging with Christ’s death and new life. Churches differ about timing and how the practice conveys that meaning.',vocab:[['Baptism','The water rite associated with Christian initiation and life in Christ.'],['Profession of faith','A person’s expressed confession or affirmation of faith.'],['Sacramental','Describing a practice as a means or sign of God’s gracious action, with meanings differing among traditions.']],deeper:'Compare the theological meaning of baptism with its timing and ritual form. A shared word can conceal real differences that deserve fair explanation.',reflect:'What question would help you understand a congregation’s baptism practice?',model:'I would ask what the congregation promises to do afterward. That connects the event with ongoing care rather than treating it as an isolated ceremony.',evidence:['Paul relates baptism to Christ’s death.','The passage connects it with walking in newness of life.','This short passage alone settles every debate about infant baptism.','A person must pass this course’s doctrine test to be welcomed.'],claim:'Baptism is interpreted through participation in Christ and a changed life.',application:'Ask how a community connects its baptism practice with ongoing support and discipleship.',decisions:[['A learner says infant baptism proves a church disregards faith. What is a fairer approach?','Ask how it understands divine initiative, nurture, and later confession.','Reduce the practice to a lack of interest in faith.','A fair comparison explains the rationale a tradition actually gives.'],['Someone treats baptism as an event with no communal responsibility. What should be asked?','How the community supports the person’s continuing life.','Only whether a certificate was issued.','The lesson connects initiation with belonging and new life.']]});

add({unit:12,id:'communion-table',title:'Communion: remembering Christ at a shared table',reading:'1 Corinthians 11:23–34',ref:[46,11,23,34],objective:'Connect Communion’s meaning with the treatment of those who gather.',body:[
'Paul recounts the meal’s connection with Jesus’ body and the new covenant in his blood. Remembering and proclaiming Christ’s death give the practice its focus. Communion is therefore more than a generic social meal, even though its social conduct also matters.',
'The surrounding argument concerns a gathering marked by division and unequal treatment. The closing instruction to wait for one another helps connect the ritual account with that problem. Reading examination only as private introspection can miss the communal harm Paul is addressing.',
'Christians differ about Christ’s presence: bodily real-presence accounts, spiritual-presence accounts, and memorial emphases are not identical. Brief summaries should not imply that a memorial emphasis means indifference or that all real-presence traditions explain the same mechanism.',
'This guide affirms an open table: all are welcome, always, without a baptism, membership, or doctrinal-agreement requirement. Other churches set different admission practices. Explain that difference clearly while letting the shared-meal context challenge humiliation and exclusion in actual conduct.'
],simple:'The meal remembers and proclaims Christ’s saving work. How participants treat one another belongs inside its meaning.',vocab:[['Communion','The Christian shared meal also called Eucharist or the Lord’s Supper.'],['Memorial','An emphasis on remembrance and proclamation, not necessarily mere mental recollection.'],['Open table','In this guide, welcome without membership, baptism, or doctrinal-agreement requirements.']],deeper:'Read the full chapter when considering examination and judgment language. The immediate problem includes the treatment of other people at the gathering.',reflect:'How could a table’s stated welcome be undermined by its arrangements?',model:'If someone cannot access the meal or is publicly shamed, the spoken welcome lacks practical credibility. I would ask what arrangement needs to change.',evidence:['Paul links the meal with remembrance and proclamation.','He also instructs the participants to wait for one another.','The passage is unrelated to how people at the gathering are treated.','Every Christian tradition explains Christ’s presence in exactly the same way.'],claim:'Communion’s proclamation and the community’s conduct must be read together.',application:'Examine whether the arrangement of a shared meal makes its welcome concrete.',decisions:[['A lesson presents only private self-examination and omits the gathering. What should be restored?','The chapter’s concern with division and treatment of others.','A claim that private reflection is always worthless.','The communal setting is necessary without excluding all personal reflection.'],['A newcomer asks whether agreement is required at this guide’s table. What fits its policy?','No; its welcome does not depend on doctrinal agreement.','Yes; the course mission determines admission.','The guide’s approved open-table commitment is explicit.']]});

add({unit:12,id:'prayer-practice',title:'Prayer: a sustainable practice without performance',reading:'Matthew 6:5–15',ref:[40,6,5,15],objective:'Use the Lord’s Prayer as a pattern of attention while rejecting prayer as performance or a guaranteed outcome mechanism.',body:[
'Jesus criticizes prayer performed for admiration. The issue is the purpose of the practice, not a prohibition of every public prayer. A private setting can also contain performance in the imagination; the passage presses the question of whom the prayer addresses.',
'The prayer attends to God’s name and reign, daily provision, forgiveness, and deliverance. Its plural language keeps neighbors within the practice. Even personal prayer can carry a concern for needs beyond the person praying.',
'The teaching about forgiveness gives prayer an ethical dimension. It should not be used to demand immediate trust in someone who continues to harm. Prayer, forgiveness, accountability, and protective boundaries can be related without being collapsed into one required action.',
'Begin with a brief, repeatable practice: read the prayer, pause over one petition, and name a concrete concern. Prayer is not a technique that guarantees healing or replaces care. A sustainable rhythm permits missed days and honest questions without turning the practice into a competition.'
],simple:'Prayer directs attention toward God, daily need, forgiveness, and others. It is a relationship and practice, not a performance score.',vocab:[['Petition','A request within prayer.'],['Formation','The gradual shaping of habits, attention, and conduct.'],['Daily bread','The prayer’s language for needed provision.']],deeper:'Compare the prayer’s plural words with a purely individual wish list. Ask how its concerns might change both attention and action.',reflect:'What brief prayer practice could you repeat without treating a missed day as defeat?',model:'I could pause over one petition in the morning and consider whose need it brings to mind. If I miss a day, I can begin again without a penalty.',evidence:['Jesus criticizes prayer aimed at public admiration.','The prayer includes provision, forgiveness, and deliverance.','Correct wording guarantees every requested outcome.','The prayer concerns only the individual and never their neighbors.'],claim:'Prayer shapes attention and dependence rather than purchasing outcomes or admiration.',application:'Choose a brief practice that connects prayer with attention to real needs.',decisions:[['A learner worries a missed day has ruined their progress. What response fits?','Resume a sustainable practice without treating it as a merit tally.','Restart only after earning back the lost days.','Formation concerns an ongoing relationship and habit, not an unbroken performance score.'],['Someone says medical care should stop once prayer begins. What is appropriate?','Allow prayer and appropriate care to accompany one another.','Treat receiving care as proof of faithlessness.','The guide does not make prayer a replacement for care or a guarantee of healing.']]});

add({unit:13,id:'creeds-reading',title:'Creeds: learning a shared confession',reading:'1 Corinthians 15:1–8',ref:[46,15,1,8],objective:'Distinguish early proclamation, later creedal synthesis, and personal assent.',body:[
'Paul recalls a message he received and passed on concerning Christ’s death, burial, resurrection, and appearances. The language gives learners an example of a community preserving a central proclamation. It is not a complete later creed, but it helps explain why shared summaries matter.',
'Creeds state central Christian convictions in a form communities can teach and confess. The historic teaching about Trinity and incarnation in this course belongs to that creedal conversation. A creed gathers an interpretation of Scripture rather than replacing the need to read it.',
'A confession can also mark boundaries, and Christians differ about the authority given to particular formulations. This course does not require a learner to recite a creed as a personal profession. Accurate understanding and personal assent are distinct outcomes.',
'When studying a creed, identify its claims about God, Jesus, salvation, the Church, and hope. Compare each with relevant biblical testimony and ask what misunderstanding a phrase seeks to prevent. Treat unfamiliar wording as an invitation to inquiry rather than a reason to silence questions.'
],simple:'A creed is a shared summary of belief. Learning what it means is different from being required to profess it.',vocab:[['Creed','A shared formulation of central beliefs.'],['Confession','An expression of faith, or a fuller doctrinal statement in some traditions.'],['Assent','Personal agreement with a claim, distinct from understanding it.']],deeper:'A biblical proclamation and a later doctrinal formula are related but distinct forms. Avoid claiming that every later term occurs verbatim in the earlier text.',reflect:'How would you show that you understand a belief you have not personally accepted?',model:'I would explain its claim and rationale in terms its adherents recognize, then state my questions separately.',evidence:['Paul recalls a message received and passed on.','The proclamation centers on Christ’s death and resurrection.','Understanding a creed is identical to personally professing it.','A later doctrinal term must appear verbatim in this passage to have any interpretive use.'],claim:'Shared proclamation provides a basis for teaching, while later formulations require explanation.',application:'Explain a confession’s claim before distinguishing your own agreement or questions.',decisions:[['A beginner can explain a creed but remains uncertain. Has the learning failed?','No; understanding and assent are distinct.','Yes; inquiry counts only if it ends in immediate profession.','The course assesses understanding while preserving space for personal questions.'],['A teacher quotes a later formula as though Paul used its exact wording. What should change?','Distinguish the biblical proclamation from later synthesis.','Remove every connection between the creed and Scripture.','Historical and literary distinctions can be kept while examining a real relationship.']]});

add({unit:13,id:'authority-unity',title:'Authority and unity across Christian traditions',reading:'Ephesians 4:1–6',ref:[49,4,1,6],objective:'Compare sources of authority and forms of church life without equating unity with uniformity.',body:[
'Ephesians calls for humility, patience, and efforts toward unity. The repeated language of one gives that unity a theological foundation. The passage does not present a modern denominational directory or resolve every later institutional disagreement.',
'Christian traditions give different accounts of how Scripture, inherited tradition, communal interpretation, and church leadership relate. Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant approaches also contain internal variety. Describing a source of authority should include how it is interpreted and by whom.',
'Church governance may involve bishops, councils of elders, or congregational decision-making. Governance and doctrine are related but not identical: knowing who makes a decision does not by itself tell us what decision they will make. Compare specific claims rather than relying only on labels.',
'Ecumenical cooperation can involve shared worship, learning, or service while disagreements remain explicit. A fair conversation asks what another tradition affirms and why, then identifies the practical implications. Unity need not require pretending meaningful differences are absent.'
],simple:'Christians can share central commitments while differing about authority and church structure. Compare the specific claims and their effects.',vocab:[['Ecumenism','Work toward relationship and cooperation among Christian communities.'],['Polity','The organization and governance of a church.'],['Tradition','Teaching and practice handed on within a community, interpreted in different ways.']],deeper:'Do not infer a congregation’s entire theology from its governance structure. Ask how a particular decision is authorized, reasoned, and practiced.',reflect:'What shared task might Christians undertake while acknowledging a disagreement?',model:'Communities could cooperate in meeting a neighborhood need while being honest about different sacramental practices. Cooperation would not require claiming the differences are unreal.',evidence:['The passage calls for humility and patience.','It names shared theological grounds for unity.','Unity requires every congregation to have identical customs.','Knowing a church’s governance automatically reveals all its doctrines.'],claim:'Christian unity is a shared calling that requires patient conduct amid real differences.',application:'Represent a neighboring tradition fairly and identify a practical area of cooperation.',decisions:[['Two churches differ in governance. What would make a comparison useful?','Ask who decides, how reasons are evaluated, and what follows in practice.','Rank their sincerity from their structure alone.','Governance describes authority arrangements, not the spiritual worth of participants.'],['A shared service project is proposed despite doctrinal differences. What is possible?','Cooperate while naming relevant differences honestly.','Pretend cooperation requires every disagreement to disappear.','Unity and cooperation need not erase all distinctions.']]});

add({unit:13,id:'traditions-welcome',title:'Seven traditions: compare claims and local practice',reading:'Romans 14:1–6',ref:[45,14,1,6],objective:'Use a fair comparison method for the seven traditions considered in this course.',body:[
'The seven traditions considered here are ELCA Lutheran, United Church of Christ, United Methodist, Metropolitan Community Churches, Episcopal, Christian Church (Disciples of Christ), and Alliance of Baptists. They are not interchangeable, and a national statement does not automatically describe every local practice.',
'ELCA teaching gives particular attention to justification and law/gospel; Methodist teaching to grace and sanctification; Episcopal identity to Anglican worship and a tradition of common prayer. These are starting emphases rather than exclusive possessions. Each community also speaks about Scripture, discipleship, and shared life.',
'United Church of Christ, Disciples, and Alliance communities give significant room to local congregational life while relating through wider structures. MCC’s public identity gives particular attention to inclusive Christian community and LGBTQ people. Use official statements to examine the details rather than turning an emphasis into a stereotype.',
'The guide’s own approved perspective is explicitly LGBTQ-affirming and open-table, with MCC and Disciples influences. Romans 14 supplies a practice of receiving others without making every difference a reason for contempt; it does not settle every modern controversy. Ask a local congregation directly about welcome, leadership, sacraments, and how questions are handled.'
],simple:'Compare official beliefs, local practice, and your actual questions. A denomination’s label is a starting point, not a complete description.',vocab:[['Denomination','A church body or family with a shared identity and organization.'],['Congregational life','The worship, decisions, and practices of a local community.'],['Sanctification','Growth in holiness and transformed life, understood differently among traditions.']],deeper:'Receiving others in Romans 14 should not be used to erase harmful conduct or all doctrinal distinctions. Identify what the ancient dispute is before drawing a modern comparison.',reflect:'Which concrete questions would help you evaluate whether a local church practices its stated welcome?',model:'I would ask whether LGBTQ people can marry, lead, and participate equally, and whether Communion has admission requirements. Specific answers matter more than a broad label.',evidence:['Romans 14 addresses differing practices within a community.','It warns against contempt and judgment among those discussed.','A denominational name guarantees identical practice in every local congregation.','Welcoming conversation requires claiming all traditions teach the same things.'],claim:'Difference calls for careful description and accountable practice rather than contempt or assumed uniformity.',application:'Compare official statements with concrete local questions about participation and teaching.',decisions:[['A church uses the word welcoming. What gives that claim practical meaning?','Ask about actual participation, marriage, leadership, and table practices.','Assume the word answers every specific question.','A broad label needs concrete practices to make its meaning clear.'],['A learner summarizes a tradition with a hostile stereotype. What should they do?','Consult its own account and distinguish official claims from local experience.','Treat one anecdote as the complete definition.','Fair comparison uses evidence and allows internal variety.']]});

add({unit:14,id:'ethical-texts',title:'Difficult ethical texts: neither avoidance nor easy excuses',reading:'Philemon 1:8–21',ref:[57,1,8,21],objective:'Distinguish a text’s explicit appeal, historical setting, and unresolved ethical questions.',body:[
'Paul appeals to Philemon concerning Onesimus through the language of love, relationship, and reception as a brother. Read the appeal’s actual wording rather than immediately replacing it with either a modern abolition statement or a defense of slavery.',
'The letter operates within a world in which slavery existed. That historical fact helps explain the situation but does not make slavery morally acceptable. Context explains a setting; it is not a machine for turning harm into goodness.',
'The appeal can be examined for how it changes the relationship it addresses and for what it leaves unstated. Christians have used biblical texts in opposing ways concerning slavery. A responsible reading must confront the harm of oppressive uses rather than claiming the interpretive history is uncomplicated.',
'Use the same method with conquest and restrictions on women: record explicit claims, examine context and competing readings, name the moral problem, and evaluate an application through Jesus’ teaching and human dignity. Some questions remain difficult after careful study. Honesty about those limits is part of the work.'
],simple:'Read what the text says and what it leaves unresolved. Understanding an ancient setting does not require excusing its harms.',vocab:[['Historical context','The circumstances in which a text and its audience are situated.'],['Ethical evaluation','Judgment about the rightness and effects of an action.'],['Interpretive limit','A boundary beyond which the available evidence does not settle a claim.']],deeper:'Philemon’s appeal and modern abolition are not identical statements. Examine the relationship between them without claiming the letter explicitly answers every later question.',reflect:'What would an honest explanation of a difficult text need to acknowledge?',model:'It would name the troubling issue, summarize the actual appeal, and distinguish a defensible implication from an explicit statement.',evidence:['Paul appeals for Onesimus to be received in a changed relationship.','The letter uses the language of love and brotherhood.','An ancient practice becomes morally good merely because it was common.','The letter explicitly supplies a complete modern abolition platform.'],claim:'The appeal presses a changed relationship while leaving ethical questions that require continued examination.',application:'Separate textual statements, implications, and unresolved moral questions when explaining a difficult passage.',decisions:[['A teacher says slavery was normal, so no ethical question remains. What is wrong?','Historical explanation has been substituted for moral evaluation.','The ancient setting was mentioned at all.','Knowing why a practice existed does not establish that it was right.'],['Another teacher claims every modern conclusion is explicitly stated in the letter. What should happen?','Distinguish the stated appeal from later implications.','Avoid reading the wording because the conclusion seems desirable.','Responsible interpretation makes its reasoning and limits visible.']]});

add({unit:14,id:'inclusion-reading',title:'Inclusion: explain disagreement without putting dignity on trial',reading:'Matthew 22:34–40',ref:[40,22,34,40],objective:'Connect the guide’s affirming commitments with a transparent method of ethical interpretation.',body:[
'Jesus names love of God and neighbor as central commands and relates them to the Law and Prophets. This provides a governing concern for Christian ethical reading. Love must be explained through conduct and responsibility rather than left as an undefined slogan.',
'This guide explicitly affirms LGBTQ dignity, relationships, marriage, and equal participation in ministry. It does not claim every Christian tradition shares that conclusion. Non-affirming readers often appeal to the continuing scope of prohibitions; affirming readers examine ancient contexts, the relationships being described, and the wider ethical witness.',
'A brief introduction cannot replace close study of every contested passage. It can teach the questions an argument must answer: what conduct is described, who is addressed, what assumptions are present, and how the proposed application relates to love and the dignity of those affected.',
'Discussion should represent disagreements accurately while refusing harassment or the demand that LGBTQ participants defend their worth. A community’s interpretation has practical consequences. Evaluate whether its stated welcome includes relationships, leadership, and equal participation rather than relying only on general language.'
],simple:'This guide is affirming. It explains how arguments are made while treating participants’ dignity and welcome as established commitments.',vocab:[['Affirming','Here, recognizing LGBTQ relationships, marriage, and equal participation in Christian community.'],['Scope','The range of conduct or people to which an argument or instruction applies.'],['Ethical principle','A guiding concern used when reasoning about conduct.']],deeper:'Love is not a shortcut that removes the need to interpret specific texts. A careful argument explains its textual reasoning and practical effects.',reflect:'How can a discussion examine an argument without making a person defend their worth?',model:'I can ask about the claim’s evidence and scope while making participation and protection from harassment explicit.',evidence:['Jesus names love of God and neighbor as central commands.','He relates these commands to the Law and Prophets.','An ethical slogan removes the need to examine specific arguments.','Respectful interpretation requires making a participant’s human worth an open debate.'],claim:'Love provides a central ethical concern that must become responsible interpretation and conduct.',application:'Discuss textual arguments while protecting equal dignity and making welcome practical.',decisions:[['A discussion begins demanding that one participant justify their worth. What should happen?','Return to evaluating arguments and protect the participant’s dignity.','Continue because humiliation makes discussion more rigorous.','Analytical rigor concerns claims and evidence, not harassment.'],['A church advertises inclusion but restricts equal participation. What question is needed?','What does its welcome actually permit in relationships and leadership?','Whether the advertisement sounds sufficiently warm.','Specific practices reveal the scope of a general claim.']]});

add({unit:14,id:'jewish-roots',title:'Jewish roots and respectful religious difference',reading:'Romans 11:1–6',ref:[45,11,1,6],objective:'Reject a simple abandonment account of Israel while distinguishing Christian witness from certainty about individuals.',body:[
'Paul asks whether God has rejected his people and answers negatively. His own identity is part of the response. This is an important obstacle to a simplistic story in which Christianity appears only after God discards Israel.',
'Jesus, Paul, and the earliest Christian movement belong within Jewish history. Judaism also continues as a living tradition with its own interpretation of Scripture. It should not be presented merely as a failed precursor whose only purpose was to prepare for someone else’s religion.',
'Christian teaching about salvation through Christ raises real questions about covenant and those of other faiths. Christians answer those questions differently. This guide teaches Christ’s saving significance without assigning a known final destiny to particular people or presenting personal speculation about repeated lives as doctrine.',
'Respectful witness includes accurate description, listening, and rejection of collective Jewish blame for Jesus’ death. A disagreement about truth does not authorize contempt or the erasure of a community’s own account of itself. Begin by learning what another person actually believes.'
],simple:'Christianity has Jewish roots, and Paul rejects a simple claim that God discarded Israel. Religious difference calls for accuracy and respect.',vocab:[['Covenant','A committed relationship involving divine promise and communal responsibility.'],['Remnant','A remaining group within a wider people, a theme used in several biblical settings.'],['Witness','Communicating one’s faith through words and conduct without claiming control of another’s response.']],deeper:'Read Romans 9–11 as a connected argument before using one sentence to settle every question about Israel. This lesson establishes a boundary against simplistic abandonment, not a complete covenant theory.',reflect:'What would respectful disagreement require you to learn before speaking?',model:'I would ask how the other person describes their faith rather than assuming my tradition’s summary fully represents it.',evidence:['Paul explicitly rejects the claim that God has rejected his people.','He identifies himself within Israel in the argument.','The passage makes Judaism irrelevant as a living tradition.','A learner can determine every individual’s final destiny from religious labels alone.'],claim:'Paul’s argument resists a simplistic abandonment of Israel.',application:'Describe another tradition accurately and avoid claiming knowledge of an individual’s final destiny.',decisions:[['A summary says God discarded the Jews when Christianity began. What should challenge it?','Paul’s explicit question and negative answer.','A broader repetition of the same slogan.','Romans 11 does not permit that simple abandonment account.'],['A group assigns eternal destinies from people’s labels. What limit matters?','The guide does not claim such knowledge about individuals.','A label supplies complete access to a person’s relationship with God.','Teaching salvation through Christ does not give the learner omniscience about people.']]});

add({unit:14,id:'suffering-discernment',title:'Suffering, miracles, and spiritual discernment',reading:'Luke 13:1–5',ref:[42,13,1,5],objective:'Distinguish theological questions from unsupported judgments about victims or illness.',body:[
'Jesus challenges the inference that people who suffered particular disasters were worse sinners than others. The examples include human violence and a falling tower. The passage does not give a complete theory of suffering, but it directly resists a particular way of blaming victims.',
'This guide distinguishes moral harm arising through human choices from disease and disasters within a dynamic natural world. God’s ability to bring good from suffering does not mean every tragedy was desired or required. Love, freedom, lament, and responsible care remain part of the discussion.',
'Biblical miracle and deliverance accounts deserve attention to their narrative claims and purposes. Christians differ over personal, symbolic, and systemic readings of spiritual evil. The course does not treat a modern illness as evidence of possession or an unhealed condition as evidence of inadequate faith.',
'When someone is suffering, respond first with attention to immediate needs, listening, and appropriate help. Leave room for prayer and unanswered questions. Do not impose an explanation of God’s hidden intention or let a spiritual label displace practical care.'
],simple:'A tragedy does not prove its victims were worse people. Care can begin before every theological question is answered.',vocab:[['Moral evil','Harm connected with wrongdoing and the use of human agency.'],['Natural suffering','Suffering connected with disease or natural events rather than a directly identified malicious act.'],['Theodicy','An attempt to reason about God’s goodness in relation to evil and suffering.']],deeper:'The passage’s rejection of one inference does not answer every philosophical question. State both what it challenges and what remains unresolved.',reflect:'What can you responsibly do when you cannot explain why a loss occurred?',model:'I can help with an immediate need and listen without claiming the loss reveals a hidden moral defect in the person.',evidence:['Jesus questions the assumption that these victims were worse sinners.','The passage names both violent killing and a falling tower.','A disaster establishes the superior guilt of its victims.','A spiritual label removes the need for appropriate practical care.'],claim:'The passage rejects using tragedy as sufficient evidence of a victim’s greater guilt.',application:'Offer care without diagnosing a hidden spiritual cause from suffering alone.',decisions:[['A person’s illness is labeled proof of weak faith. What should be challenged?','The unsupported inference from suffering to spiritual failure.','The person’s willingness to seek practical help.','Suffering alone does not establish the claimed spiritual diagnosis.'],['You cannot solve the philosophical problem of evil. What remains possible?','Listening, prayer if welcome, and concrete help.','Withholding help until an explanation is certain.','Care does not depend on possessing a complete explanation.']]});

add({unit:15,id:'resurrection-body',title:'Resurrection: transformed life rather than escape',reading:'1 Corinthians 15:35–49',ref:[46,15,35,49],objective:'Explain Paul’s continuity-and-transformation imagery without reducing resurrection to a ghostly existence.',body:[
'Paul responds to questions about how the dead are raised and what kind of body is involved. The seed image introduces both continuity and transformation. The image helps readers think, but it is not a detailed biological mechanism for the resurrection.',
'The contrasts include perishable and imperishable, weakness and power, natural and spiritual. Spiritual body should not automatically be translated into no body at all. Within the historic teaching used here, resurrection concerns transformed embodied life.',
'Christian hope does not make present embodied life disposable. The claim that God overcomes death is different from the claim that physical existence never mattered. Care for bodies and grief at death can coexist with hope.',
'Resurrection is also distinct from reincarnation into repeated earthly lives. The course teaches the historic resurrection hope and does not incorporate personal recurrence theories as scriptural fact. Explain the distinction directly while acknowledging limits on what these images specify about future life.'
],simple:'Paul speaks of transformed embodied life. His images hold change and continuity together without mapping every future detail.',vocab:[['Resurrection','God’s raising of the dead into renewed life.'],['Imperishable','Not subject to the decay described in the passage’s contrast.'],['Continuity','A connection between what exists now and what is transformed.']],deeper:'An image can illuminate transformation without explaining its mechanics. Ask which feature of the comparison serves Paul’s argument.',reflect:'How can hope for resurrection support care for embodied life now?',model:'If embodied life matters to Christian hope, care for a suffering body is not a distraction from spirituality. It belongs within the concern.',evidence:['Paul uses a seed comparison when discussing resurrection.','The passage contrasts perishable and imperishable conditions.','Spiritual body necessarily means no embodied life at all.','The seed comparison provides a complete scientific mechanism.'],claim:'Paul’s imagery presents transformed life with both continuity and difference.',application:'Explain what an image illuminates without claiming it specifies every future mechanism.',decisions:[['A learner says bodily care is irrelevant because hope means becoming a ghost. What needs correction?','The reduction of resurrection to disembodiment.','Only the timing of becoming a ghost.','Historic resurrection teaching affirms transformed embodied life.'],['A diagram claims to show the exact mechanics from the seed image. What limit matters?','An analogy explains selected features rather than every mechanism.','Every detail of seed biology must predict the future body.','The function of the comparison controls how far it can be pressed.']]});

add({unit:15,id:'judgment-mercy',title:'Judgment: serious teaching with interpretive limits',reading:'Matthew 25:31–46',ref:[40,25,31,46],objective:'Take judgment and mercy seriously while distinguishing the text from certainty about particular people.',body:[
'The scene presents judgment through a king’s separation of groups and their treatment of people in need. Its dramatic form makes conduct toward others impossible to dismiss. Christian teaching about hope includes accountability, not only reassurance.',
'The identity of the least of these and the passage’s relation to wider salvation teaching are debated. A responsible explanation should state the reasons for a reading and compare it with other texts. It should not silently replace the whole Christian account of grace with a score of good deeds.',
'Christians also differ in how they understand final punishment. Eternal conscious separation or punishment, final destruction, and universal reconciliation are distinct interpretations whose arguments draw on different passages and readings of key language. Describing these does not make this course adopt every view.',
'The guide teaches salvation through Christ and the seriousness of judgment without declaring the final destiny of particular individuals. Its practical direction is attentive care, repentance, and hope. Avoid using speculation about the future to evade a need directly in front of you.'
],simple:'Judgment makes care and accountability serious. Explaining its interpretations is different from claiming to know each person’s final destiny.',vocab:[['Judgment','God’s evaluation of life and conduct.'],['Universal reconciliation','An interpretation expecting the eventual reconciliation of all, disputed among Christians.'],['Final destruction','An interpretation in which final punishment ends in destruction rather than endless conscious existence.']],deeper:'Compare Matthew 25 with the grace passages in Unit 9. Explain how your reading relates them instead of letting one isolated formulation cancel the other.',reflect:'What can this scene challenge without giving you authority to pronounce an individual’s fate?',model:'It can challenge my indifference to someone’s need. I can respond to that challenge without claiming certainty about another person’s eternal condition.',evidence:['The scene connects judgment with treatment of people in need.','Its groups respond with surprise to the king’s account.','The reader is given complete knowledge of every individual’s final destiny.','This scene makes examination of other salvation passages unnecessary.'],claim:'The scene gives care and accountability weight while requiring interpretation within a wider scriptural conversation.',application:'Attend to a concrete need without claiming an authority to pronounce another person’s final destiny.',decisions:[['A reader uses the scene to calculate who is saved from a few visible deeds. What is missing?','Its relation to wider teaching and the limits of human knowledge.','A larger public scoreboard.','The passage should not become an invented method for knowing individuals’ final destiny.'],['Someone says interpretive disagreement makes the passage ethically irrelevant. What remains?','Its urgent challenge concerning the treatment of others.','Permission to ignore people in need.','Unresolved details do not remove the text’s demand for serious attention.']]});

add({unit:15,id:'apocalypse-hope',title:'Apocalypse: symbols directed toward hope',reading:'Revelation 21:1–5',ref:[66,21,1,5],objective:'Read renewed-creation imagery in relation to divine presence rather than headline prediction.',body:[
'The vision describes a new heaven and earth and a city descending from God. Its direction resists a simple story in which hope consists only of leaving creation behind. The imagery joins renewed life with God’s dwelling among people.',
'Tears, death, mourning, and pain are named in the promise of their end. The vision’s hope speaks into the reality of suffering rather than pretending suffering never mattered. It gives final renewal a relational and embodied significance.',
'Revelation also contains messages to congregations, symbolic conflict, worship, and judgment. Read those forms together. Numbers and beasts should not be confidently assigned to current personalities simply because a contemporary comparison feels striking.',
'Compare the tree of life in Revelation 22 with Genesis 3 in Unit 3. A thread can connect beginnings and endings while changing the setting and function of an image. Trace that development, then ask how hope supports faithful life now rather than obsessive prediction.'
],simple:'The vision points to renewed life with God and an end to suffering. Read its symbols in context before applying them to a headline.',vocab:[['Apocalypse','An unveiling conveyed through visionary and symbolic forms.'],['New creation','The renewal of life and creation within Christian final hope.'],['Allusion','A reference or echo connecting a text with another passage or image.']],deeper:'The return of an image is a literary connection, not proof that every physical detail must be identical. Compare what each passage does with it.',reflect:'How can hope for renewal affect one present responsibility?',model:'It can strengthen care for a person in grief rather than encourage indifference to their present pain. Hope does not require dismissing what they have lost.',evidence:['The city is described as descending from God.','The vision names the end of death and tears.','Every striking headline comparison identifies a symbol’s certain referent.','Hope requires denying the importance of present suffering.'],claim:'Renewed-creation imagery joins divine presence with the ending of suffering.',application:'Connect hope with present care while declining unsupported predictions.',decisions:[['A headline is declared the certain fulfillment of a beast image. What should be examined?','The image’s literary setting and evidence for the proposed link.','Only how dramatic the resemblance sounds.','A resemblance is not sufficient evidence for a confident identification.'],['Someone dismisses grief because the future will be renewed. What does the vision itself name?','The tears and death whose end is hoped for.','A requirement to pretend no suffering occurred.','The promise takes suffering seriously rather than denying its reality.']]});

add({unit:16,id:'project-observe',title:'Project lab 1: observe a whole book',reading:'Philemon 1:1–7',ref:[57,1,1,7],objective:'Build a book profile that separates observations from assumed background.',body:[
'Begin with a manageable book and read it completely in Explore. Philemon works well for a first project because its short length permits repeated reading. The selected opening introduces names, relationships, an address, and thanksgiving; the full letter supplies the appeal.',
'Build a profile with five fields: summary, people, opening move, authorial claim, and audience. Record what the book itself says separately from traditional attribution or scholarly reconstruction. A familiar title is not always a complete account of authorship or audience.',
'Locate the book on the shelf, identify its group, and distinguish narrative setting from composition date. These are different forms of information. The integrated Content, Groups, Order, and Chronology missions reinforce these distinctions across all 66 books.',
'Your evidence notebook should include references and at least one observation that surprised you. Do not fill a missing field with a guess to make the profile look complete. Mark an uncertainty and identify what source or passage could help investigate it.'
],simple:'Read the whole book, build a profile, and label what you know versus what you still need to investigate.',vocab:[['Book profile','A concise record of a book’s content, people, opening, authorial information, and audience.'],['Observation','A statement about what the text actually presents.'],['Reconstruction','An account of missing context inferred from evidence.']],deeper:'A salutation can name participants without explaining every relationship or circumstance. Keep the profile open to correction by the rest of the letter.',reflect:'What uncertainty would you leave visible in your first book profile?',model:'I would separate the people explicitly named from an inferred account of the gathering. A question mark is more useful than an invented fact.',evidence:['The opening names people addressed in the letter.','It introduces thanksgiving and relational concerns.','Every missing historical detail should be filled with a confident guess.','Shelf position and composition date are the same kind of information.'],claim:'A useful profile separates textual observations from inferred background.',application:'Create a referenced book profile with at least one explicitly labeled uncertainty.',decisions:[['A profile contains an audience claim without a reference. What should happen?','Locate its textual or external evidence and label any inference.','Keep it because a complete-looking profile is more impressive.','An evidence record should expose rather than conceal uncertainty.'],['The reader has studied only the salutation. What comes before a whole-book summary?','Read the full letter in Explore.','Assume the opening already contains the complete argument.','The opening introduces a work; it does not replace the work.']],sequence:['Build a responsible first-pass project workflow.',['Read the complete book.','Record a profile with references.','Separate observations from inferences.','List an uncertainty to investigate.'],'Gather the text before completing and qualifying the profile.']});

add({unit:16,id:'project-interpret',title:'Project lab 2: trace an argument and a theme',reading:'Philemon 1:8–16',ref:[57,1,8,16],objective:'Construct an interpretation from evidence and compare a plausible alternative.',body:[
'Read the appeal after completing the whole-letter profile. Identify what response Paul seeks and how the relationship language supports it. Build an argument map rather than collecting phrases that happen to sound relevant to a preferred conclusion.',
'A map should connect observations to a claim and make the warrant for each connection visible. A statement can support a limited interpretation without establishing every larger ethical conclusion. Include at least one piece of evidence that complicates your first reading.',
'Then trace one of the course’s fourteen themes into another book. Mercy in Philemon can be compared with mercy in a parable, for example, but the two settings must remain distinct. A thread is a developing connection, not permission to make every passage say the same thing.',
'Write a short alternative interpretation that an informed reader could recognize as fair. Explain what evidence favors your reading, what remains uncertain, and what would change your mind. The integrated Themes and Content missions supply broader practice; this project requires you to articulate reasons.'
],simple:'Connect evidence to a claim, test a fair alternative, and trace a theme without erasing the passages’ differences.',vocab:[['Warrant','The reason evidence supports a particular claim.'],['Counterevidence','Evidence that challenges or complicates an interpretation.'],['Theme','A concern traced across passages whose development must be examined.']],deeper:'A connection is not proved by a repeated English word alone. Ask how the theme functions in each passage and whether the comparison clarifies or distorts it.',reflect:'What evidence might make you revise your first reading of the appeal?',model:'I would look for a phrase that limits what I assumed Paul was demanding. I should explain that limit rather than omit it from my summary.',evidence:['Paul makes an appeal using relational language.','The passage asks the recipient to regard Onesimus differently.','A theme comparison allows every literary setting to be ignored.','A persuasive interpretation should conceal details that complicate it.'],claim:'Interpretation requires explaining how evidence supports a claim and where its force is limited.',application:'Write a supported interpretation with a fair alternative and one condition for revision.',decisions:[['A theme study finds the same English word in two books. What comes next?','Compare how the concern functions in each context.','Assume the passages have identical meanings.','Repeated wording alone does not establish an identical argument.'],['A detail weakens your preferred interpretation. What makes the project stronger?','Include it and refine the claim.','Remove it so the argument appears cleaner.','An accountable interpretation makes counterevidence visible.']]});

add({unit:16,id:'project-apply',title:'Project lab 3: explain, apply, and revisit',reading:'Philemon 1:17–25',ref:[57,1,17,25],objective:'Complete a five-part study brief and evaluate a proportionate application.',body:[
'The closing appeal includes reception, possible debt, confidence, and further personal relationships. Read these details within the whole letter before claiming its implications. A project should finish with more contextual understanding than it had at the beginning.',
'Write a five-part brief: question, context, evidence, interpretation with an alternative, and application with a limit. A short brief can be rigorous if references and reasoning are clear. Length alone is not evidence of understanding.',
'Choose a concrete response and identify whom it affects. Distinguish a justified application from the passage’s direct wording and check whether it risks recreating harm. An action that sounded loving in abstraction may need revision after listening to the people involved.',
'Return later and explain the book without looking at the first draft, then compare and correct it. Use the verse lab to rebuild a passage and identify its speaker and recipient. Independent study is successful when you can reason, revise, and continue learning; the app does not certify the truth of a private theological conclusion.'
],simple:'Finish with a clear evidence-based brief, a responsible action, and a plan to revisit your understanding.',vocab:[['Synthesis','A connected explanation that brings several observations and distinctions together.'],['Proportionate application','A response no broader or more certain than its supporting reasons justify.'],['Retrieval practice','Recalling material before checking and correcting it.']],deeper:'The app checks the study method, not the quality of every private essay. A trusted discussion partner can supply feedback that an automated completion milestone cannot.',reflect:'What would you ask a reviewer to challenge in your finished brief?',model:'I would ask whether my evidence supports the claim and whether the proposed application overlooks someone affected by it. I would revise rather than defend the draft at all costs.',evidence:['The closing appeal includes reception and possible debt.','It remains embedded in named personal relationships.','A longer brief is necessarily a better interpretation.','A completion badge certifies every private theological conclusion.'],claim:'A finished study makes its evidence, interpretation, and practical limits reviewable.',application:'Share a clear argument for feedback when appropriate and revise the application in light of its effects.',decisions:[['Your application burdens a person whose needs you did not consider. What should you do?','Revisit the plan with their experience in view.','Keep it because changing an application shows weak faith.','An application should remain accountable to its actual effects.'],['You earn the project milestone. What does it establish?','Completion of the method tasks, with further feedback still useful.','Certification that every conclusion in your private notes is correct.','Automated completion and independent evaluation are different outcomes.']],sequence:['Assemble the five-part final brief.',['State the question.','Describe the context.','Present referenced evidence.','Explain an interpretation and a fair alternative.','Propose an application and state its limit.'],'Make the reasons visible before proposing the response.']});
})();
